  "description": "Faithful replication with the original renderings: 18 Lumion renderings of the same standard waterfront scene, identical except for the aquatic plant assemblage at the water's edge, built from the paper's three viewing types, flowers (F1-F6: Canna glauca, Nelumbo nucifera, Pistia stratiotes), leaves (L1-L6: Cyperus papyrus, Arundo donax 'Versicolor', Nymphoides peltata) and flowers and leaves (FL1-FL6: Iris tectorum, Thalia dealbata, Nymphaea tetragona), each as single-species, two-species and three-species assemblages of emergent, floating-leaved and free-floating plants. Participants first answer the background section (gender, age group, education, major or profession, and whether they prefer viewing flowers or foliage) and one overall aesthetic rating of aquatic plant landscapes, then see the 18 renderings one at a time with the paper's prompt (Imagine you are physically present in the landscape shown; please rate it according to your personal perception.) and rate each on the paper's eight items from 1 = not at all to 7 = very much: landscape beauty perception (The plant landscape in the photograph is aesthetically pleasing.) and seven visual preference items, plant landscape preference, ornamental value, visual richness, vertical layering, colour, spatial arrangement and naturalness. Rebuilt from Zhang, Lai, Ye, Zhou, You & Dong (2025), Balancing Landscape and Purification in Urban Aquatic Horticulture: Selection Strategies Based on Public Perception, Horticulturae 11(9): 1044, with the renderings cropped from its Figure 3 and the items from its Table 3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: F1-F6, L1-L6, FL1-FL6 as in its Table 2; ratings are keyed by image id with item codes LBP, PLP, APO, PLVR, PLL, PLC, PLS, PLN. Analysis as in the paper: scenic beauty estimation (SBE), with each participant's beauty ratings standardised (Z = (R - mean) / SD) and averaged per rendering, comparison of SBE by viewing type and species richness, and multiple stepwise linear regression of beauty perception on the seven visual preference items for each viewing type; the paper (320 valid online responses, snowball sampling) found flowering assemblages rated highest (F6 0.487, F3 0.480, F2 0.267) and foliage ones lowest (L5 -0.367, L1 -0.349), flowering > mixed > foliage, ornamental value the main driver for foliage landscapes and visual richness for flowering ones. Differences from the original study: (1) the wording of the background questions and of the overall aesthetic rating is ours, because the paper lists only their topics; the age groups follow the paper's reported groups (under 18, 18-25, 26-35, 36-45, 46-60) with an over-60 group added, and gender, which the paper reports but does not list among the questions, is asked as well; (2) the paper gave a short explanation of each attribute before each rating block, which it does not print, so none is shown; (3) the paper does not state the order of the renderings, so here they are shown in individually randomized order; (4) the paper's questionnaire was in Chinese on Questionnaire Star; here the English wording of its Table 3 is used and anyone can take part; (5) the renderings are the figure copies (about 1230 px wide).",
